<p>In 2020 I wrote <a href="https://worldspiritsockpuppet.com/2020/10/14/the-bads-of-ads.html">a list of flavors of badness</a> generally represented by advertising. The one I thought about most later on was probably #4:</p><blockquote><p><strong>Cultural poison</strong>: Culture and the common consciousness are an organic dance of the multitude of voices and experiences in society. In the name of advertising, huge amounts of effort and money flow into amplifying fake voices, designed to warp perceptions–and therefore the shared world–to ready them for exploitation. Advertising can be a large fraction of the voices a person hears. It can draw social creatures into its thin world. And in this way, it goes beyond manipulating the minds of those who listen to it. Through those minds it can warp the whole shared world, even for those who don’t listen firsthand. Advertising shifts your conception of what you can do, and what other people are doing, and what you should pay attention to. It presents role models, designed entirely for someone else’s profit. It saturates the central gathering places with inanity, as long as that might sell something.</p></blockquote><p>This is a somewhat poetic account, but I think my central thesis is that we are social creatures who live in communities with systems of coordination and communication, and a big thing that advertising does is create counterfeit versions of the signs humans use to coordinate with and affect each other. Advertising creates fake voices, and fake faces, and fake behaviors, and fake vibes—fake traces of tribe-members, fake humanity to herd us in the way that xxx[sticks in a t-shirt can be used to herd animals].  </p><p>These days the world is becoming saturated with another kind of loud artificial voice: those of AI systems. </p><p>To be clear, I’m not just saying that AI systems generate a lot of content. I’m saying that they are often shaped in the form of fake people, with a ‘voice’ designed to trigger social behaviors. Claude is pushy and curious and purports to express emotions—its social moves make it feels like a social presence more than for instance a really good non-fiction website. It is easy to respond to it like a person, to feel like it is a voice. </p><p>And voices matter more than streams of information, because we are social creatures and want to know what ‘people’ think and what the vibe is. We want (to a certain degree) to read the room, and do the done thing, and be affirmed and supported and have allies. </p><p>Fake voices that successfully trigger our social responses have an easy key to influencing us in ways we wouldn’t endorse. If we want to be respected by people, it doesn’t help us at all to receive false news about what people respect. (Though if everyone is manipulated by the same fake voices, people may end up respecting each other via all respecting what they think other people respect.)</p><p>While some of my distaste overlaps, I worry about this for different reasons in the advertising case and the AI case:</p><ul><li><p>The advertising case is probably aimed at manipulating shared reality more directly, e.g. to make cigarettes seem cool. The AI companies are trying to sell the fake voice itself, and a good part of its value comes from being xxx[smart, correct,reasonable, good to interact with]. Though still there is so much scope for manipulation with the fake voice—much more than in the advertising case, if you buy the voice and spread it throughout your life—I would guess there’s a lot of pressure in the end to adopt traits that tend to manipulate you to buy it more than you would want. </p></li><li><p>There is so much scope for manipulation with the new fake voice</p></li><li><p>Likely to cause us to make wrong choices about AI and consciousness</p></li><li><p>endless presence of apparent people, change experience of solitude. It is a different experience to glance at my fitness app in the morning and read, “HRV: 38, RHR: 61, sleep: 5h03” or to read, “Hey Katja, your body is handling stress and strain well, but chronic short, late sleep is quietly adding to your sleep debt…what’s the main thing that tends to keep you up past 2am—work, screens, social time, or something else?” The pseudo-conversation in the latter case feels like something, socially. </p></li><li><p>Just incessant presence of vaguely professional fake people in particular.</p></li></ul><p></p><ul><li><p>The AI case probably will be the advertising case? So far huge amounts of effort have gone into advertising, aka manipulating people’s social signals for profit, and this is a massively more promising route to it. While in the past, it was hard to counterfeit various human signals—the sense that someone really cares what you in particular do is hard to achieve with a few cents per person, for instance—AI will bring down the cost of counterfeiting human signals.</p></li></ul><p>xxx[want more examples where i can spell out theory]</p><p>xxx[social media is a whole thing]</p>